Propagate joint distributions and control the numerical approximation (@met-monte-carlo: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @metrology, #source "../sources/GUM-Monte-Carlo.lingua#r_79V20QP3T2KZ6GD07SK5TGZ1J4", #source "../sources/Uncertainty-Software.lingua#r_0JJ7DQ8KRD6ACMN16THF87F3PW", #source "../sources/GUM-and-Amendment.lingua#r_3KA76CK3QAA3M470TC3XHP19MQ", #metrology "Multivariate-and-constraints.lingua#r_4V8WXSM2RD4S9XTX4X1R1Z6P6A", #reproducible-computation "../../reproducible-computation/numerics/Random-streams-and-task-identity.lingua#r_1QGP0E4XMM77C1FWNHWDHJ2TJT") { r_5NYXBSGVDB0Q2V5VPJ7JDWRH1E
**Read:** [Propagation of distributions using Monte Carlo — JCGM 101](../sources/GUM-Monte-Carlo.lingua#r_79V20QP3T2KZ6GD07SK5TGZ1J4), the complete guide, including conditions, numerical control, and examples; [Uncertainty Machine and its numerical methods — Lafarge and Possolo](../sources/Uncertainty-Software.lingua#r_0JJ7DQ8KRD6ACMN16THF87F3PW), the complete manual and one worked example; [GUM — JCGM 100:2008 and the 2026 nonlinearity amendment](../sources/GUM-and-Amendment.lingua#r_3KA76CK3QAA3M470TC3XHP19MQ), the nonlinearity amendment.

Monte Carlo propagation samples from an explicitly specified joint input distribution, evaluates the model for each draw, and summarizes the resulting output sample. It can expose asymmetry and nonlinear behavior that a local approximation misses. Its validity still depends on the model, input information, and representation of dependence.

Keep the algorithm inspectable: define distributions and their parameters; generate joint inputs; evaluate the stated model; inspect invalid or physically impossible evaluations; estimate the chosen output summaries; and assess numerical stability at the precision being reported. Never silently discard inconvenient draws, because doing so changes the implied distribution.

Use an exact benchmark. Let X be uniform on [0,1] and Y = X². Then P(Y ≤ y) = √y for 0 ≤ y ≤ 1, E(Y) = 1/3, and var(Y) = 4/45. The median is 1/4. A 95% equal-tail interval is [0.025², 0.975²] = [0.000625, 0.950625]. Evaluating the model at the mean input gives 1/4, which is not the output mean.

**Work:** reproduce this distribution by simulation and compare mean, standard deviation, median, and interval endpoints with the exact values. Vary simulation size and random seed or independent runs. Explain numerical error in a tail quantile separately from measurement uncertainty. Then reproduce the correlated sum and difference benchmark from [Propagate uncertainty with sensitivities and covariance](Sensitivity-and-correlation.lingua#r_0EF35BB106S2F2ATJBXBR49DFR) using joint inputs.

For an actual measurement, preserve software/version, model expression, distributions, correlation construction, simulation settings, and convergence evidence. The NIST application is an optional independent check, not the definition of the method. An implementation that produces many digits but fails the simple benchmarks needs correction before its complex result is trusted.

**Continue in reproducible computation:** [Give stochastic work explicit stream and task identities](../../reproducible-computation/numerics/Random-streams-and-task-identity.lingua#r_1QGP0E4XMM77C1FWNHWDHJ2TJT).

**Continue:** [Several results share information and sometimes exact constraints](Multivariate-and-constraints.lingua#r_4V8WXSM2RD4S9XTX4X1R1Z6P6A).

**Return:** [Measurement and metrology](../Metrology.lingua#r_3G54ENPA974K19AB5XF0P641N5).
